00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.860
By the end of this module,

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.660
you'll be able to
do three things.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.410
First, you will construct KQL
statements using the standard

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:51.570
pattern that Sentinel and log
analytics expect; second,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.680
you'll use KQL to search log
files for a security event

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.450
so you can find the signals
in the noise; and third,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.730
you will filter searches based

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.230
on meaningful investigation
criteria, like event time,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:11.220
severity, domain, and any
other relevant field,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:18.510
so you can narrow quickly from
everything down to this matters.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.960
KQL query are
read-only requests.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.900
You're asking the
system to process data

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.790
and return a result, not to
modify the underlying data.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.130
The language is
written as plain text,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.600
and follows a data flow model
that is designed to be easy

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.780
to read, write, and automate.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.360
In practice, you'll build
a query that starts

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.520
with a dataset, usually a table,
and then you stream that dataset

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.040
through a sequence of operators.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:54.330
It also helps to remember
the schema organization.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:59.040
KQL works with entities
arranged in a hierarchy similar

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.670
to SQL database,
tables and columns.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.270
The most important mental
model, though, is the pipe.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.280
Everything to the left of a
pipe symbol is processed first

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.630
and the result then flows to the
right into the next operator.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.610
If you understand that flow,
you can read almost any query

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.840
like a story, start here,
refine this, compute that,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.680
and then return the final
shape of the result.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.230
During demonstration,
you'll typically begin

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.500
by accessing a Log
Analytics demo environment

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.980
and working directly
in the query window.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.670
And if you want additional
support as you practice,

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.720
Microsoft provides a KQL
quick reference and a set

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.820
of query best practices

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.670
in the Azure Data
Explorer documentation.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.100
Let's start with
the simplest way

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.980
to query, referencing a table.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.620
In Sentinel you'll
commonly work with tables

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.710
like security events,
security alerts.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.000
Most of what you write will be
a tabular expression, columns

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.340
and rows, statements,
meaning the input is a table

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.890
and the output is also a table.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.520
When you chain operators
together using pipes,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.720
passing the tabular result
from one step to the next,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.230
at the most basic level
you could run a query

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.840
by entering only the table name,

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.090
but that approach is
usually inefficient,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:37.620
because you'll pull back far
more data than you need.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.130
The real power comes
from immediately shaping

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.260
and filtering what you retrieve.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.890
When you need to quickly
hunt for a term across data,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.070
the search operator is
a fast way to begin.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.280
For example, you might
use something as simple

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.110
as "Search 'New'" to find
records containing that word.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.080
You can also sculpt the
search to a specific table

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.310
like searching within "Security
Event" and any table that starts

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:11.460
with "App" by using
the pattern such as

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.820
"Search Event, App," "New".

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.840
This is especially useful
early in an investigation

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.100
where you're still figuring

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.580
out where the relevant
evidence might live.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:30.510
Most investigations rely heavily
on the "Where" operator.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:34.170
Because filtering is how you
get from broad telemetry

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.840
down to events that matter.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.800
A common first filter
is "Time," for example,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.490
starting with "Security Event"
and then narrowing to "Event,"

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.810
where time generated is
within the last hour using

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:54.630
"Time generated greater than
ago 1h," as in one hour.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:58.170
From there, you can combine
condition into a single step,

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.090
such as filtering the last hour

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:06.300
and also matching an event ID
like, "Event ID equal, equal,

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:10.530
double quote, 4624,"
for a logon event.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:15.720
You can also express the same
logic as multiple "where" steps,

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.300
which can be easier to read.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.750
First filter by time,
then by event ID,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.480
then maybe by account
type, for example,

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.110
using the case insensitive
equality operators,

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:32.370
the equal tail, with
a value like "User".

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.070
And when you want to
match several event IDs,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:40.620
you can use "In," such as
filtering, "Where event ID in,"

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:46.860
and then specify the event ID,
4624, 4625, as an example,

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.850
put them in bracket for
successful and failed login.

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.650
Using the LET statement.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:58.860
As your query grows,
you'll want them

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.770
to be easier to
read and maintain.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.950
And that's where the LET
statement comes into play.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:09.600
With LET, you can declare
variables and reuse them.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.410
For example, you might design,
"Time offset equals --

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:19.950
" what age, what hour, and,
"Discard Event ID equals 4688,"

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.520
then filter "Security Event"
to a specific window,

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.580
events between time ago, time
offset times two, so two hours,

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:34.350
and ago time offset also
excludes a noisy event ID

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.980
by checking that this event
ID, exclamation mark,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.500
equal discarded event ID,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:45.450
so not equal event --
discarded event ID.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:49.560
You can also use LET to
define a reusable dataset.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.290
For example, you could
build a table called

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:57.450
"Low Activity Accounts" by
summarizing count by "Account",

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:03.480
and then keeping only accounts
with fewer than 1,000 events.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.680
After that, you can reference
the low-activity account

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.840
like any other table and further
filter, such as finding account

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.320
where the name contains
the string "SQL".

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.100
This pattern helps you
declare and reuse variables,

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.130
and also declare
dynamic table or lists

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.980
that can simplify
complex hunting logic.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.260
Next let's create new
fields on the fly

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.450
with the "Extend" operator.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:37.260
"Extend" adds calculated
column to your result,

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.400
which is incredibly useful when
you need to derive a value

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.180
that doesn't exist
directly in the data.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.370
For example is starting
with security events,

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:53.210
filtering on the last hour and
ensuring both process name

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.770
and "Process aren't empty."

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.570
From there you can compute a
new column called "StartDir,"

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.540
by using the
"Substring" function

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:07.800
and "String Size" function
to strip the process name

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:11.070
down to just the starting
directory portion.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.370
Another powerful pattern
is normalizing value

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.140
into a sortable order.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.630
For example, using
security alert

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:24.210
over the last seven days you
can extend a new column called

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:29.250
"Severity Order" using the
"Case" function, mapping high

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:34.830
to three, medium to two, low to
one, and informational to zero,

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.770
and using minus
one as a default,

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.380
for example, for missing values.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.800
This makes it much
easier to sort, group,

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:51.450
or threshold alert consistently
in later steps in the demo.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:55.380
Once you filter and enrich
your dataset, you often need

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.990
to sort it to spot patterns.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:02.100
The "Order By" operator
does exactly that.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:07.470
Building on the previous example
where we computed "StartDir,"

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:12.750
you can order the result with
"StartDir Descending", D-E-S-C,

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:16.800
and then "Process
Ascending," A-S-C.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.300
Sorting like this can quickly
reveal unusual directories,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.660
rare processes, or
anything that stands

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.870
out once the noise
has been reduced.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.900
Another essential set of
tools is the "Project" family

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.800
of operator, which controls
the shape of your output.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.060
With "Project," you choose
which column is included

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.180
in the output, and
you can rename, drop,

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.540
or answer
newly-computed columns.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.360
If you want to exclude
a certain column,

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.670
you can use "Project Away".

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:57.120
If you want to keep
only a specific column,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.250
use "Project Keep".

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:02.880
To rename fields cleanly,
use "Project Rename".

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:07.170
And if you want to just reorder
the columns in the output,

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.360
use "Project Reorder".

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.440
For example, you might start
a security event and simply

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.150
"Project Computer" and
"Account" to focus your view.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.630
Or you can take a longer
query filtering by time,

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:26.340
ensuring process fields are
present, extending "StartDir",

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.950
sorting the result,
and then finish

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.620
by removing a
verbose column like

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:34.620
"Process Name" using
"Project Away".

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.240
This is a great habit for making
your result easier to read

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.490
and easier to share
with your teammate.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.020
With the fundamentals in place,

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:50.580
let's move into analyzing
query results with KQL.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:56.640
This is where KQL becomes more
than "Find Event," it's a way

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:02.820
to summarize what's happening
and communicate it clearly.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.340
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:08.340
to summarize data
using KQL statement

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:13.260
and render visualization
using KQL statement.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.040
These capabilities help you
answer high-level questions

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.470
like, "How often is this
happening, who is most impacted,

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:26.310
and is the situation
getting better

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:30.000
or is it getting
worse over time?"

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.220
The "Summarize" operator is
the heart of analysis in KQL.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:37.980
It groups records and
calculate aggregates

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.790
so you can understand
patterns at scale.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:46.620
Common functions include "tell,"
"count if" to count records,

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:51.120
"dcount" and "dcount if" to
estimate distinct value.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:54.810
We've got "average" and
"average if" for average,

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.660
"max" and "max if" for maximums,

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.270
"sum" and "sum if"
for a total value.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:03.480
For a quick example, you
could take security events

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:06.000
from the last hour and
summarize a number

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:11.640
of distinct IP addresses using
"Summarize Distinct IP".

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:15.780
In a more investigation-focused
scenario,

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:20.790
you might define a timeframe,
like 30 days, and a threshold

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:24.090
that queries sign-in logs
for sign-in attempts

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:28.260
where the result description
contains invalid password.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.840
From there, you can summarize
the distinct number

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:34.320
of applications being
targeted by counting

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.990
"Distinct App
Display Name" value

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:41.100
or "User Principal Name"
and "IP Addresses".

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.500
And finally, filter to a case

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:48.330
where the application count
meets or exceeds your threshold.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:52.740
This kind of query helps
highlight suspicious patterns

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:57.850
such as password spray
activity across many apps.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.980
Beyond counting and
distinct values,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.200
"Summarize" can help you filter

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.610
by keeping only the most
relevant record in each group.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:13.230
Two key functions are
"Arg Max," "Arg Min."

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.110
For example, if you
filter a security event

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:22.800
to a specific host like
"SQL10.na.contosohotels.com,"

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:28.890
you can use "summarize
arg_max(TimeGenerated,*)

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:33.900
by Computer" to keep the most
recent records by computers;

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:37.470
or you can use
"arg_min(TimeGenerated,*)

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:43.000
by Computer" to keep only
the earliest record.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:48.330
Sometimes the goal isn't
just to calculate a number,

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:53.550
it's to prepare unstructured
output so you can reuse or pivot

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:59.010
on to function to help our
"make_list" and "make_set".

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:02.100
For example, you might
query "Security Event"

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:06.750
for the last hour, filter
to successful logon with

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:13.320
"Event ID equals 4624," and then
summarize "Make List Account

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:18.570
by Computer," to create a list
of accounts seen on each host.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:21.240
If you want unique values only,

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:25.240
you can use
"Make_set(Account)" instead.

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.810
Once your data is
summarized, you can turn it

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:34.650
into visual using the
"Render" operator.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:39.180
Common visualization includes
area chart, pie chart,

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:43.140
column chart, scatter
chart, time chart.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.900
For example, you could
take the security events

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:50.220
over the last hour,
summarize count by account,

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:53.070
and then render a
bar chart to see

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:56.180
which account appears
more frequently.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:59.750
Or you can summarize count
over time using a bin,

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:04.490
such as "bin(TimeGenerated,
1m)," meaning one minute,

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:06.350
and then render a time chart

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:11.580
to visualize activity
trend minute by minute.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:15.450
Now, let's move beyond
single-table query and focus

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:19.410
on building multi-table
statements using KQL.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.750
In a real Sentinel
investigation,

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:26.070
the story is often spread
across multiple data sources.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.590
So you need to merge
and correlate table

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:30.940
to see the full picture.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.880
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.820
to create query using
unions to merge results

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:41.900
from multiple tables and you'll
be able to merge two tables

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:43.970
with the "Join" operator.

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.760
These two techniques,
using "Union"

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:51.680
and "Join" are foundational
for correlating activities

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:56.940
across identity logs and
point events, alerts,

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.810
and application telemetry.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:03.420
The "Union" operator
lets you combine rows

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:08.580
from multiple tables into a
single unified result set.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.380
As a simple example is

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:15.360
"SecurityEvent / union
SigninLogs," which pulls events

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:18.390
from both sources
into one stream

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.810
so you can continue the process.

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:24.500
Once you've unioned
tables, you can summarize

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:27.410
across the combined
dataset, for instance,

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:31.220
running "Summarize Count," and
then projecting that count

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:34.710
into a clean output column.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.860
You can also union in
a more advanced way,

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:41.820
like unioning a base table
with the summarized output

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.130
of another query in parentheses.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:47.460
For example, unioning
"Security Event"

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:51.240
with a summarized count
from sign-in log.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:54.810
Another common technique
is using wild card such as

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:59.550
"Union App*" and then
summarizing count by type

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:02.520
to understand how many
records are coming

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:06.490
in from each matching table.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:12.660
While union stacks result,
"Join" correlates them.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:17.190
The "Join" operator combines
records from two datasets based

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.490
on a matching key,
such as account name

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:24.270
and a device name, a
session identifier.

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:28.200
In this example, we started
with "Security Event" filtered

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:32.970
to log-on event, for example,
where Event ID is 4624,

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:37.080
then summarized a
log-on count by account

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:40.830
and project log-on
count and account.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:45.830
Next we join, using
"Kind equals inner,"

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:50.000
which a second dataset
built from log-off event,

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:56.010
where Event ID equals 4634,
summarizing a log-off count

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:59.310
by account and
projecting the same key.

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.370
Joining one account
gives a combined view

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:08.850
where you can compare log-ons
and log-off side by side.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:12.930
When you join table, a specific
join kind, sometimes called

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:16.200
"Join flavor" determines
which record are kept

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:20.790
on the left side, the right
side, or both, it's essential

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.460
to understand the
impact of that choice.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:28.350
With some join kinds you'll keep
only the matches with the other,

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:31.560
you'll preserve unmatched
record from one side.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.360
As you build correlated query
for investigation, always pause

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.900
and confirm what should
happen to records

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.060
that don't have a match.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.600
That's one decision that can
completely change the conclusion

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.490
you draw from the data.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:54.330
Next, we'll focus on
working with string data,

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:59.280
also known as text, plain
text, using KQL statements.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:04.830
Security data often contains
key information embedded inside

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:10.800
text, things like command line,
account, strings, JSON blob,

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:12.630
and even event message.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:14.010
So being able to extract

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:17.940
and transform text
is a major advantage

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:22.140
in hunting an incident response.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:25.770
After completing this module,
you'll be able to extract data

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:29.100
from unstructured
field, extract data

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:34.740
from structured string data,
and create functions using KQL.

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:37.800
These skills help
you turn nasty text

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:42.600
into a clean field you can
filter, summarize, and join,

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.680
just like any other columns.

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:50.100
To pull value out of
unstructured text,

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:53.760
KQL provides the
"Extract" function,

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.460
which uses regular expression.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:59.790
A simple illustration
is printing the result

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:07.530
of the example we have onscreen
and confirming it returns 45.6.

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:12.360
That pattern defines a
regex, regular expression.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:15.780
Choose a capture
group, the brackets,

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:18.060
and apply it to a string.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:21.870
Scale directly into
real security data.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.170
For example, you might
query security event

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:30.180
for a privileged activity
event, like Event ID 4672,

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:34.860
and focus on a record where
"Account Type" equals "User".

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.190
From there you can create
a normalized account field

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:43.410
by extending a new column, such
as "Account Name," and using the

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:47.310
"Extract" function
with a regex designed

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:50.490
to pull just the
username portion

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:53.640
from a different account
format, converting the input

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:56.010
to lowercase for consistency.

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:58.920
Once the account
name is extracted,

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:02.940
you can summarize a login
count by account name,

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.840
exclude empty result, and
then filter to account

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:09.720
with fewer than,
let's say, 10 logins,

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:13.470
an approach that can help
highlight rare privilege login

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:15.790
worth investigating.

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:21.150
Another approach for
unstructured string is the

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:23.850
"Parse" function, which is great

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:26.970
when the text follows
a consistent pattern.

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:31.110
In this example, we create
a small data table called

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:34.770
"Traces," with a column
named, "Event Text,"

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.670
containing a structured
message like, "Event:

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:44.250
NotifySliceRelease," as we
see onscreen over here.

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:49.290
When we parse event text,
mapping part of the string into

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:52.860
"Name" field like, "Resource
name," "Total slice,"

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:58.410
as a long slice number, as a
long, which is a 64-bit value,

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:04.020
and "Time Stand," such as log
time, release time as a date,

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:07.770
and previous log time as
a date, after parsing,

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.740
we project those
extracted fields

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:16.540
so the output becomes a
clean table you can analyze.

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:21.660
Now let's take a look at
structured string data,

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:25.440
especially dynamic
fields and JSON.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:29.610
In sign-in logs, some properties
are already available

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:31.530
as dynamic object.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.990
So you can extend
fields directly.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:38.850
For example, you can extend
a new column OS from the

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:43.950
"Device Detail" columns,
"DeviceDetail.operatingSystem"

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:48.300
to quickly analyze sign-in
by operating system.

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:52.080
When JSON in stored as a
string, you can convert it

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:56.700
into a dynamic object
using "parse_json."

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:02.160
For instance, you might extend
the "AuthDetail" by parsing the

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:06.420
"Authentication Detail"
field, then extracting value

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:10.350
from the first elements,
like "AuthMethod" from

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:16.140
"AuthDetail[0]," authentication
method, and "AuthResult"

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:20.790
from a nested field, such as
"AuthDetails[0]," the first one,

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:23.220
and then the field name

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:26.760
"Authentication Step
Result Detail".

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:31.260
Finally, you can project
"AuthMethod," "AuthResult,"

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:36.280
and default "AuthDetailObject"
to validate your parsing.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:41.130
In some scenarios, you'll
want to enrich your query

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:46.740
with external data, like a
watchlist of user ID hashes

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.550
or known indicators
stored outside Sentinel.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:55.320
KQL supports this with the
external data capability.

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:59.580
For example, you might query
a user table and filter

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:02.940
where user ID is
in a list returned

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:07.110
by your external
data user ID string.

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:10.920
Pulling value from a text file
holds it an Azure Blog Storage.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:15.390
In practice, access to that blob
typically requires a secret

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:19.080
token, such as a SaaS
token, so always plan

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:24.460
for secure access control when
integrating external sources.

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:32.010
To make your KQL reusable, you
can save queries as functions,

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:34.380
especially useful for parsers

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:37.140
and common
investigation pattern.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:41.430
For example, you might start
from, "Security event filtered

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:46.950
by event ID equals 4672 and
account type equals user,"

00:27:46.950 --> 00:27:52.140
and then save the query as a
function name privileged login.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:55.710
After it's saved, you can
invoke it by simply calling

00:27:55.710 --> 00:28:01.120
"PrivLogins," just like you
would reference a table.

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:05.850
Now, it's time to bring
everything together

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.040
in a focused demonstration.

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:12.630
Here we'll create and run KQL
query in Microsoft Sentinel,

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.420
applying the patterns
we've seen so far.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:21.990
In this demo, let's take a look
at Log Analytics workspace,

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:28.020
how to query with KQL through
our defender workspace.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:32.160
First thing I'll do here is
crack open Microsoft Sentinel,

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:36.420
open our workspace, and under
"Log," enter the query hub.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:41.610
So let's select "Sentinel,"
let's go into "Logs."

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:43.830
Let's close the query hub.

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:45.600
This is useful to get started,

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:47.880
but we're going to
get started raw.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:52.230
This brings us to the query
history in "Simple Mode."

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.510
Let's switch to "KQL Mode".

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:59.370
KQL mode will allow us
to write raw KQL query,

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:02.100
great for learning,
great for understanding.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:06.330
It's also, in my opinion, the
fastest way to write query.

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:07.680
On the left over here,

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:10.890
we can select tables
we'd like to query.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.320
But in our case, I'll just
start typing the name

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.240
of the table I want to query.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.410
Let's go with "Security Events".

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:22.290
This is about the simplest
query you can create.

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:26.310
Type in the name of a table,
hit the "Run" button, boom,

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:29.370
here's a list of
results for you.

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:34.920
We're seeing all of the rows in
our Security Events CL table.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:36.840
Actually, we're not
seeing all the rows

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:39.150
because there are
filters at the top.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:42.810
We can see we're only showing
the first 1,000 results

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:48.000
and the last 24 hours as well.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.810
If necessary, we can change the
time range or choose the number

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:53.940
of results that show up.

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:57.360
We can do that either
through the toolbar

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:00.840
or we can do it directly
inside the query interface,

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.970
which we'll see later on.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.460
Another way that we can query --

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:08.910
start a query in KQL
is by doing a search.

00:30:08.910 --> 00:30:16.410
Let me give you an example
over here search, "Computer."

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:20.940
What this does over here is it
will take a look at every table

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:24.000
and every entry,
and pull up results

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.840
that have the word,
"Computer" in them.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:28.770
Here we go.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:35.460
Notice the dollar sign table
in the columns over here.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:39.240
That tells me which table
see the results come from.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.120
Some come from the
office activity,

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:45.160
others from security event.

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.090
We can see why those rows were
included, they have the word,

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:53.850
"Computer" in them
under the account.

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:56.130
When it comes to the one
for office activity,

00:30:56.130 --> 00:30:59.040
would be somewhere
else in the entry.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.380
Well, let's take a look at
a variant on the search.

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:06.000
We're going to say now search in
"Security Event" and any table

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:09.270
that begins with "App," App*,
and we're going to look

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:12.240
for the word, "New" this time.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:17.710
Let's run the query, and
here are our results.

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:23.310
Again, same principle,
because it's a search,

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:26.370
it's going to break up
a variety of table,

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.500
look in all tables
that we've selected.

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:32.100
We can see which table
the results we're seeing

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:36.640
over here come from using the
dollar sign table column.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:41.250
Now, let's start
adding some filter.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:46.200
We know that we can filter using
time range at the top over here.

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:50.850
But let's filter right in the
core, very useful especially

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.940
if you're going to save
queries and reuse them.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:57.750
So we're going to search
query "Security Event,"

00:31:57.750 --> 00:31:59.700
but now we're going
to add a predicate

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:06.150
where time generated
greater than "ago 7 days."

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:11.910
So let's go back one week,
so from now to one week ago.

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.300
Notice because we are now
adding a predicate based

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:21.000
on time generated, time range
now says, "Set in query."

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:22.410
Here are the results.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:26.760
So now we are seeing the
last seven days of activity

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:29.520
in "SecurityEvent_CL".

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:31.920
We can filter further.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:36.480
For example, in the table, we
have an "Event ID" column.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:38.500
Let's filter by that one.

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:45.840
I'll add an "and clause"
over here and "Event ID,"

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:53.220
and we're going to put in two
equals and let's go with 4624.

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:57.930
Run event, and now we're looking
at the last seven days of events

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:03.150
and all the event
IDs that are 4624.

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:04.350
To make it easier to read,

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:06.990
let me put that on
two separate rows.

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:09.990
So we'll have one row
to filter the duration,

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:13.320
another row to
filter by event ID.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.800
Makes it a little
bit easier to read.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:19.800
We're going to add another
word clause over here,

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:24.750
where we're going to search
by account, by account type.

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:28.020
And let me use a different
operator this time.

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:32.340
I'll use the equal tail
and the word, "User."

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:36.870
The equal tail does a
case insensitive search.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:40.890
Notice over here how the
word, "User" has a capital U,

00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:44.550
but in the query, I wrote
it with a lowercase U.

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:46.620
So that's a good
operator to know.

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:52.540
It's an operator you'll see very
often in sample KQL queries.

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:57.420
Another useful operator
I'd like to introduce you

00:33:57.420 --> 00:33:59.940
to is the "In" operator.

00:33:59.940 --> 00:34:03.480
What if I'd like to look
at multiple event IDs,

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:09.360
not just 4624, but
say, 2645 as well?

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:12.900
Let me use the "In"
operator where Event ID

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:17.730
in an inside bracket, let me
put in all the values I'd

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:21.270
like to use, 4624, 4625.

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:25.050
There we go, now we've
got both event IDs coming

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:27.890
up in the result.

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:30.470
We could also negate the
condition by saying,

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:36.810
"Where Event ID not in," and
provide a list of events.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.300
Now, as our queries get
more sophisticated,

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:42.540
we'll want to make it easier
for us to make changes

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.810
to values we provide in here.

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:48.150
And I'd like to
explore with you how

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:50.400
to use variables
in your queries.

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:52.470
Here's a quick example for you.

00:34:52.470 --> 00:34:55.150
Let me drop that in here.

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:59.850
And notice in this query we're
adding two LET statements,

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:03.000
"Let -- time offset
equals 10 minutes,

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:08.220
let discarded Event
ID equal 4688."

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:09.330
And following that,

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:13.140
we are querying our
security event CL table.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:14.970
But what's different now is

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.590
where we have our
condition like,

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:21.030
"Where time generated greater
than ago time offset,"

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:26.130
time offset is our variable
we've defined as a name

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:29.610
that we've chosen to
represent 10 minutes.

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:31.500
There it is.

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:33.480
So we don't have to repeat it,

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:36.900
we are creating a
one-minute window at 10

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:41.970
and 11 minutes here, so we don't
have to repeat 10 minutes.

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:43.800
And furthermore, if
we want to change it,

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:48.880
there's only one place where we
need to go now, the variable.

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:54.780
Let's run the query and see
that while the same will apply

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:59.670
to discarded event IDs,
"Discarded event ID" is now

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:03.390
at the top, making
it easy to change.

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:08.670
There we go, here is the result
of our query, time-generated.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:10.770
We're looking at
just one minute,

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:13.620
10 minutes ago, 11 minutes ago.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:20.190
And the event ID we should see
every event ID except 4688.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:24.750
We're using the exclamation mark
equal over here, not equal,

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:28.560
so give me everything
except 4688.

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:33.210
So this gives us a convenient
way to improve the readability

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:37.650
of our query, make it
easier to change our query,

00:36:37.650 --> 00:36:42.000
avoid duplication when
we modify the query

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.470
when we write the query.

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:48.950
Let's continue our
exploration of KQL.

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:50.300
Now let's take a look at how

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:55.170
to summarize an aggregate
result in our query.

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:57.540
Instead of seeing
individual rows,

00:36:57.540 --> 00:37:01.650
I'd like to see
aggregates of those rows.

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:04.650
So we're going to start with our
same table, "Security Event".

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:11.430
We're going to clamp it to seven
days a week and Event ID 4688.

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:13.860
But notice the next line
now, we're adding the

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:18.540
"Summarize" operation,
"Summarize count by computer."

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:20.250
So what I'd like to
know over here is

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:25.860
in our security event how many
times has a computer shown

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:27.930
up in here?

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:29.310
Let's run the query.

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:30.600
There's our result.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:33.270
We could see over here which
for our computers show

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:39.180
up more often, less often,
based on the count by computer

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:41.680
that we have over here.

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:49.380
We can see the
VictimPCContosoAzure has a lot

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.800
of activity, giving us a sense
that hey, maybe we'll want

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:56.490
to take a look at this machine.

00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:59.460
Notice as well how
"count" is spelled

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:01.320
with an underscore over here.

00:38:01.320 --> 00:38:03.480
We can change the
name to "column".

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:06.330
For example, I'll use "CNT".

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:09.540
I cannot use the word, "Count,"
because that's a built-in word.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:14.250
But let me say, "CNT equals
count by account type,"

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:20.040
and notice that the count column
is now simply known as "CNT,"

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.340
or any other name I
would've chosen to select.

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:27.330
There is the account
type, the computer,

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:29.160
and the count of events.

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:32.880
So now we're looking at the
account type, the computer,

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:36.330
and how many times does
that combination come

00:38:36.330 --> 00:38:38.430
up in the last seven days,

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:44.210
so "user@victimPC"
shows up 10 times.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:46.430
Here's a variant for you.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:50.030
Let's explore a new
query with sign-in logs

00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:53.450
where time generated is one
week ago, but now we're going

00:38:53.450 --> 00:38:58.320
to summarize not by count but
by dcount, distinct count.

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:02.670
I want to know how many distinct
IP addresses do I have,

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.400
and in this case,
in my sign-in logs,

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:09.480
I can see that only one
IP address is used.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:12.300
That's what the one
represents over here.

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:15.930
If I saw two, that would mean
there are two different IP

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:19.770
addresses that shows
up in that log.

00:39:19.770 --> 00:39:24.090
Let's continue to refine this.

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:27.690
Here's a slightly more
sophisticated query over here.

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:31.800
We're still going to use dcount,
and we're going to use variables

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.970
at the top going back,
in this case, 30 days.

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:38.490
We're going to look for
a result description,

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:41.940
the result description
column that has the text,

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:45.960
"User account is disabled,"
and we're in the column.

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:50.160
And now we're going to summarize
using dcount over here.

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:55.040
We're going to name the column
"Application count equals dcount

00:39:55.040 --> 00:39:58.640
at display name," so
let's display the number

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:02.300
of times a specific
application name shows up,

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:03.770
and we're also going to group

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:10.070
that by user principal
name and by an IP address.

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:14.690
And then we're going to add
a filter to the result.

00:40:14.690 --> 00:40:19.550
What we care about is any
application count whose name

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:22.830
shows up one or more times.

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:25.650
And there we go, in this
case, there is only one.

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:31.470
We can see that John with their
IP address has one application

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:33.180
showing up in here.

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:38.640
John's entry with that specific
address may actually show

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:42.120
up multiple times in the logs.

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:44.130
But because we're using dcount,

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:48.720
it shows us that this specific
combination exists only once,

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:53.030
shows up only once in our log.

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:57.920
Last example I'd like
to give you over here.

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:02.180
Let's talk about "make_list"
and "make_sense".

00:41:02.180 --> 00:41:04.340
That may seem
intimidating at first,

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:06.960
but I highly recommend
play around with them,

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:08.700
make sure you're
familiar with them.

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:12.300
Here's an example,
"SecurityEvent_CL".

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:14.460
Again, we're going to
go back seven days.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:17.910
We're going to look
at Event ID 4624,

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:20.220
but now summarized
by "make_list".

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:22.920
So we're going to
create a little table,

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:26.730
a sub-table over here,

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:27.780
"Make_list(Account_s)
by computer."

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:31.410
So we could see over here a
list of computers and beside

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:34.410
that we have a list
of all accounts

00:41:34.410 --> 00:41:37.020
that were found
on that computer.

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:41.160
We can expand those
list accounts to see

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:44.790
which accounts are
used by this computer.

00:41:44.790 --> 00:41:48.000
So this is a table
within a table.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:50.580
We're creating a
list of accounts.

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:55.530
Duplicate accounts will show up
multiple times in the table.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:56.880
Let's expand it.

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:00.440
And notice how we can see
the same account more

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:02.060
than once in the list.

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:04.620
Let's crack that open again,

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.450
and notice

00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:10.980
"CONTOSOAZURE/AATPService"
shows up a couple of times.

00:42:10.980 --> 00:42:12.090
That's "make_list".

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:15.540
"Make_list" does not
remove duplicate,

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:19.710
it shows us all
individual value.

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:21.990
But what if I don't care
about the duplicate,

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:26.040
I just want to know if
that account is present

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:30.660
on that system, I want
to get rid of duplicate.

00:42:30.660 --> 00:42:33.720
Well, let's make one quick
change to our query.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:37.410
Let's use "make_set"
instead of "make_list".

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:42.330
Same thing, but the difference
now is all duplicates will

00:42:42.330 --> 00:42:43.500
be removed.

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.320
Let's crack open our Victim PC.

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:47.880
Let's take a look
at the account.

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:53.430
And notice now how
"CONTOSOAZURE/AATPService" shows

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:56.490
up only once, even
though we know

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:58.950
that it's there multiple
times in the table.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:02.550
So "make_list" shows, me
all the individual values,

00:43:02.550 --> 00:43:07.300
"make_set" shows me
only distinct values.

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:12.690
For our last set of KQL demo,

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:15.660
let's get into something
a little bit fun.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:18.690
Let's get into visual.

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:21.690
We'll start with our
security events.

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:23.520
We've seen that a
couple of times.

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:27.970
We're going to filter
by the last seven days.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:35.530
There is a result,
nothing really new.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:43.680
But what I'd like to do next
is let's summarize count

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:48.240
by account, so let's see
which accounts were used

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:49.620
in the last seven days.

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:50.730
Here is the result.

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:53.970
But what I'm seeing over here
is the name of an account

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:56.430
and the number of
times they're used.

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:59.070
I'm going to change the
sort over here order

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:01.350
by account going descending,

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:04.770
the account that has the most
activity down to the one

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:06.990
that has the least activity.

00:44:06.990 --> 00:44:08.940
Administrator is at the top,

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:12.510
which is always a
bit concerning.

00:44:12.510 --> 00:44:16.020
But what I'd like to do over
here is to make it easier for me

00:44:16.020 --> 00:44:20.130
to read and evaluate, understand
what's happening over here,

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:22.560
especially in terms of ratio.

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:25.560
Let's generate a visual.

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:28.500
Let's render a bar chart.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:32.910
Let me add over here at the
end, "Render bar chart."

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:38.610
And there we go, KQL now
generates a bar chart for us,

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:41.820
much nicer visual, allowing
you to get a good sense

00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:46.560
as to really how much more
the administrator account is

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:48.720
being used.

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:51.570
And if I'd like to look at
it more from a ratio side,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:55.480
how about we render
a pie chart instead?

00:44:57.450 --> 00:45:00.600
And there we go,
here's our pie chart.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:02.760
Again, administrator
represents, what,

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:08.130
42.97% of all account activity.

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:11.820
Not too surprising, but
maybe a bit concerning.

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:15.060
Either there's privilege, abuse,

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:18.300
or maybe something
else is happening.

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:20.790
But one way or another,
we've seen over here how

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:25.570
to generate visual using KQL.

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:29.130
Let's recap what
you've accomplished

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:30.660
in this learning path.

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:33.180
You have learned how to
construct KQL statement

00:45:33.180 --> 00:45:36.330
from Microsoft Sentinel,
building queries

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:38.700
that follows the dataflow model.

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:43.800
You practiced key KQL operators,
like "Summarize," "Render,"

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:48.390
"Union," "Join," and "Extend"
to filter, shape, analyze,

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.730
and visualize your result.

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:54.780
You also learned how to
extract data from unstructured

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:58.860
and structured text fields,
which is critical for working

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:01.590
with real-world
security telemetry.

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:05.340
And finally, you saw how to
create functions and "Parser,"

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:10.870
using KQL so your best
queries can be reused.

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:16.830
As we close out, take a moment
to reflect on the key outcome.

00:46:16.830 --> 00:46:22.410
You now have practical KQL
skill you can apply immediately

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:24.450
in Microsoft Sentinel.

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:27.750
Whether you're hunting,
triaging alerts,

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:30.510
or investigating an incident,

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:34.140
the more you practice writing
queries and reading the pipeline

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:37.920
from left to right, the
faster you'll be able to move

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:42.180
from raw logs with
confident solution.

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:43.950
This concludes our session.

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:48.060
There are many more ways to
continue your learning journey.

00:46:48.060 --> 00:46:52.410
We encourage you to watch other
videos in Discourse or search

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:59.980
out your next favorite topic on
Microsoft Learn at aka.ms/learn.

